= Guaraná

_Ayurvedic status: not classical. Chinese: not classical. Greco-Roman / European: not classical (one early-modern naturalist mention). The authoritative tradition is_ indigenous Saterê-Mawé _of central Amazonia (Maués, Amazonas state), the original cultivators and ritual users of guaraná, with broader Brazilian_ caboclo _folk medical use and modern caffeine pharmacology. This entry, like the açaí entry, replaces the standard "Greco-Roman" section with an "Indigenous Amazonian / Brazilian folk medicine" section in the same scholarly register._

== Nomenclature & Etymology

*Latin:* _Paullinia cupana_ Kunth, family Sapindaceae; the cultivated variety is _Paullinia cupana_ var. _sorbilis_ (Mart.) Ducke (the Saterê-Mawé domesticated form, the Brazilian commercial source). A climbing woody vine of central Amazonia; the drug is the *seed* (with its red-orange arillus removed at maturity, or partially retained — the half-cracked seed with white aril remnants is the iconic image, and the basis of the indigenous "eye" naming). _Paullinia_ commemorates Simon Pauli (1603–1680), Danish royal physician and herbal author; _cupana_ is from indigenous Tupi-Guarani _kupana_. The synonymized species _Paullinia sorbilis_ Mart. (Martius's earlier name) is the basis of the variety epithet.#footnote[Species identity: standard botanical authorities for Sapindaceae (_Flora do Brasil_); the _P. cupana_ vs. _P. cupana_ var. _sorbilis_ distinction is between the wild type and the Saterê-Mawé domesticate. _Paullinia_ eponym: Burkhardt, _Eponymisches Wörterbuch der Botanik_; Simon Pauli's _Flora Danica_ (1648) was Pauli's principal herbal work. _Sorbilis_ from Latin "drinkable" (referring to the preparation).]

*English:* _Guarana_ (also _guaraná_, with the accented Brazilian Portuguese spelling). From Brazilian Portuguese _guaraná_, from Tupi-Guarani _wara'ná_, in turn from Saterê-Mawé (Tupi family) — the Saterê-Mawé are the original Amazonian cultivators and the etymological source. The folk gloss connects _warana_ to the Saterê-Mawé phrase for "fruit like the eyes of the people" (referring to the cracked-open seed with red aril and white endocarp — exactly resembling a black-pupiled eye on a red-and-white field). The "eye" reading is consistent with the well-known Saterê-Mawé origin myth of guaraná as the eyes of a divine boy buried after death and giving rise to the first guaraná plant.#footnote[_Oxford English Dictionary_ (s.v. "guarana, n."); Saterê-Mawé etymology and the "eye" reading: ethnolinguistic and ethnobotanical literature on the Saterê-Mawé (e.g. Lorenz, _Guarana: The Energy Seeds and the Saterê-Mawé_; Henman's classic monograph on guaraná). The origin myth is widely cited in Brazilian ethnographic literature.]

*Chinese:* No classical entry. Modern: 瓜拉那 (guālānà, phonetic transliteration); occasionally 巴西可可 (bāxī kěkě, "Brazil cocoa") — misleading, since the species is unrelated to cacao.

*Sanskrit:* No classical entry. No Sanskrit name.

*Tupi-Guarani / Saterê-Mawé:* _wara'ná_ — the Saterê-Mawé canonical name. The Saterê-Mawé people of Maués (Amazonas) and the Andirá-Marau Indigenous Land are the original domesticators of _P. cupana_ var. _sorbilis_ and the bearers of the ritual and medical tradition. The preparation as roasted-pounded seed paste (Saterê-Mawé _içapó_, Brazilian Portuguese _pasta de guaraná_ / _bastão de guaraná_) is the classical traditional form.#footnote[Saterê-Mawé etymology, ethnobotany, and ritual usage: Henman, _Guaraná: A História e o Mito_ (1986); Atroch and Saterê-Mawé Council, _A Cultura do Guaraná na Terra do Povo Saterê-Mawé_; the IGP / SwissAid-funded studies of the Saterê-Mawé guaraná economy. The Saterê-Mawé hold a geographic indication (IG) on guaraná production under Brazilian intellectual-property law — a recognition of the indigenous origin.]

== Classical Chinese Medicine (中醫) View

_No classical Chinese entry; placement below is reasoned cross-system, not citation._

By reasoned placement: sweet (甘) with a strong bitter (苦) and astringent (澀) note; warming (溫) — appropriate to the central-nervous-system stimulant pharmacology and to the caffeine-class profile; Heart (心), Liver (肝), Kidney (腎) channels by inference. Reasoned actions: 提神醒腦 (raises spirits, awakens the mind), 行氣 (moves qi), 利水 (mild diuretic), 開胃 (opens appetite — though traditional use is often appetite-suppressing in fatigued workers). Pharmacologically guaraná sits in the same class as coffee, tea, kola — the *caffeine-bearing* stimulant axis — and the Chinese reading would parallel that of coffee (modern Chinese 咖啡). *Cautions:* yin-depleting and qi-dispersing on prolonged use; aggravates Liver-yang rising, Heart-fire, and insomnia patterns; unsuitable for vacuity-heat or yin-deficient constitutions. All interpretive, not classical.#footnote[The reasoned placement follows the standard Chinese-pharmacy reading of caffeine-bearing stimulants (coffee, tea, kola) and is offered for cross-system comparison only. Not classical Chinese attribution; explicitly flagged.]

== Ayurvedic Dravyaguna (द्रव्यगुण)

_No classical Sanskrit entry; placement below is reasoned cross-system extension._

*Reasoned rasa:* Tikta (तिक्त, bitter — strongly, from caffeine and tannins) — predominant; Kaṣāya (कषाय, astringent — substantial, from the proanthocyanidin tannins); Madhura (मधुर, sweet — subdued, only in the prepared form with the lengthy roasting).

*Reasoned guṇa:* Laghu (लघु, light), Rūkṣa (रूक्ष, dry), Tīkṣṇa (तीक्ष्ण, sharp/penetrating); secondarily Vyavāyī (व्यवायी, quickly spreading — the stimulant onset).

*Reasoned vīrya:* Uṣṇa (उष्ण, warming) — strongly so; the stimulant warming is the defining character.

*Reasoned vipāka:* Kaṭu (कटु, pungent).

*Reasoned prabhāva:* None classical (no canonical entry). On a reasoned basis, the *prolonged stimulant effect compared with equivalent caffeine in coffee* — well documented in modern pharmacology and traditionally noted by Brazilian users — could be argued at prabhāva level (an action not predictable from caffeine content alone, but from the slow-release modulation by the tannin matrix). Explicitly interpretive.

*Reasoned doṣa effect:* Pacifies Kapha (the laghu-rūkṣa-tīkṣṇa profile is the Kapha-clearing axis); aggravates Vāta (the laghu-rūkṣa and the rajasic stimulant property dries and disturbs Vāta) and Pitta (the uṣṇa-tīkṣṇa profile inflames Pitta). The classical reading would place guaraná as a Kapha-clearing rajasic stimulant — closely paralleling coffee (kāpītha / modern _kāphī_).

*Reasoned karma:* _Medhya_ (intellect-promoting — by the modern caffeine pharmacology), _kaphahara_ (clears Kapha), _dīpana / pācana_ (kindles digestion in modest amounts), _mūtrala_ (mild diuretic), _hṛd-uttejaka_ (cardiac stimulant — modern), _nidrā-nāśana_ (sleep-destroying — the principal Saterê-Mawé / folk attribution and the modern pharmacology converge here), _śrama-hara_ (relieves fatigue — the central traditional and modern use); in excess or chronic use, _rasa-kṣaya_ (depletes the body fluids), _vāta-prakopa_ (disturbs Vāta), and _hṛd-spandana_ (cardiac arrhythmia — modern). All reasoned.#footnote[The reasoned dravyaguna placement closely parallels how modern Ayurvedic practitioners place coffee — _kāphī_ / _kāpīphala_ — in the Vāta-Pitta-aggravating Kapha-clearing rajasic-stimulant register. P. V. Sharma's _Dravyaguṇa-vijñāna_ vol. II discusses coffee similarly. Explicitly flagged as reasoned, not classical.]

=== Viruddha (विरुद्ध — incompatibilities)

_(tagged by source tier — S = core saṃhitā, N = later nighaṇṭu, F = folk/regional, M = modern/pharmacological)_

No classical viruddha (no canonical entry). The cautions are folk and modern, dominated by the caffeine pharmacology.

- *(F) Saterê-Mawé / Brazilian folk:* traditional Saterê-Mawé use is *ritually and medically calibrated* — the morning preparation, ceremonial use, and dose are governed by community knowledge. Caboclo and broader Brazilian folk practice often pairs guaraná with carbohydrates or sweetened beverages to moderate the stimulant onset.
- *(M) Modern / pharmacological — the dominant tier for this substance:*
  - *Caffeine pharmacology applies in full.* Guaraná seed is ~2–8% caffeine by dry weight (much higher than coffee bean's ~1–2%) — the *single highest natural caffeine content* among major commodity stimulant plants.
  - *Pregnancy:* like coffee, caffeine intake should be limited in pregnancy (≤200–300 mg caffeine/day, current obstetric guidance); guaraná-based products are easy to exceed this limit unintentionally.
  - *Cardiac arrhythmia / hypertension:* contraindicated in poorly-controlled hypertension, arrhythmia, or angina.
  - *Anxiety disorders / panic disorder:* commonly worsens.
  - *Insomnia and sleep disorders:* unsurprisingly aggravates; the duration of action is *longer* than equivalent coffee (the prolonged release from tannin-bound caffeine).
  - *Bleeding risk / antiplatelet:* caffeine and tannins have mild antiplatelet activity; clinically modest but relevant in concert with anticoagulants.
  - *Drug interactions:* CYP1A2 substrate competition (caffeine pathway), MAO interactions (theoretical, weak), ephedrine-class combinations (combination cardiovascular toxicity — relevant to "energy drink" formulations).
  - *Cardiotoxicity in energy-drink contexts:* documented case reports of arrhythmia, myocardial events, and hepatotoxicity in heavy energy-drink users — typically combination products (guaraná + caffeine + taurine + ephedra historically + alcohol) — flagged for *combination products* rather than guaraná alone.
  - *Pediatric:* caffeine load is high; pediatric use is not appropriate.#footnote[Caffeine pharmacology is standard pharmacology textbook material; guaraná-specific caffeine content and the slow-release tannin-bound caffeine point: published phytochemistry and pharmacokinetic studies (verify against current literature). Pregnancy caffeine guidance: ACOG, NICE, EFSA — verify current limits (200–300 mg/day is the current consensus ceiling). Energy-drink cardiotoxicity: well documented in cardiology and emergency-medicine literature; usually attributable to combination products. All (M) cautions are explicitly flagged as modern, not classical.]

== Traditional Arabic & Islamic Medicine (Ṭibb al-ʿArabī wa-l-Islāmī / Unani)

Guaraná is *not classical in Tibb*. The species _Paullinia cupana_ is exclusively Amazonian and was unknown to the medieval Arabic-Persian medical world. Modern Arabic and Persian commercial usage is the transliteration *guarānā* / *ġwārānā* (غوارانا). Reasoned modern Unani placement: the caffeine pharmacology would place guaraná in the *qahwa* (قهوة, coffee) Tibb register — Hot and Dry in the 2nd degree; *muqawwī al-rūḥ al-ḥaywānī*, *muqawwī al-dimāgh*, *muḥarrik* — but with extreme caution given the very high caffeine load (substantially higher than coffee). Coffee itself enters Tibb only in the 16th c. (Yemenite Sufi adoption, then Ottoman spread); the medieval canon is silent on it. Guaraná is post-classical-of-the-post-classical — even further removed from the medieval Tibb framework than coffee is. No substantive medieval Tibb tradition; the modern Arabic-language energy-drink and herbalist literature treats it by extension on the coffee axis.

=== Chinese medicine (中醫)

No classical Chinese formulas; modern functional-food / energy-drink use only.

=== Ayurveda

No classical Ayurvedic formulas. Some modern Ayurvedic products outside India incorporate guaraná as a substitute for or addition to traditional _medhya_ herbs (brahmī, śaṅkhapuṣpī, jyotiṣmatī, mañjiṣṭhā); this is modern Western-adoptive Ayurveda, not classical.

=== Indigenous Amazonian / Brazilian folk medicine

This is the substantive tradition.

- *Saterê-Mawé pasta / bastão de guaraná (içapó):* the canonical traditional preparation. Ripe fruits are de-pulped; seeds are roasted slowly over a low fire for hours; the roasted seeds are pounded with water in a mortar (or originally between stones) into a thick paste, then molded into hard cylindrical sticks ("bastões") and dried/smoked for storage. Use: the dried stick is *grated on the dried bony tongue of the pirarucu fish* (_língua de pirarucu_ — _Arapaima gigas_) into a cup of water, yielding a deep-brown, bitter, slightly foamy drink. The pirarucu-tongue grater is the iconic Saterê-Mawé / Amazonian implement.
- *Ritual / ceremonial use:* the Saterê-Mawé treat guaraná as a sacred plant and use it ritually at communal gatherings, important decisions, work parties, and ceremonies; the morning preparation and consumption is governed by community elders.
- *Caboclo / riverine Brazilian folk:* daily morning or workday drink; used for *cansaço* (fatigue), *dor de cabeça* (headache, especially of fatigue origin), *libido* (the popular "aphrodisiac" reputation — modest evidence in modern pharmacology), and as a digestive aid.
- *Commercial Brazilian guaraná drinks:* a major Brazilian cultural product since the early 20th c.: *Guaraná Antarctica* (1921, Companhia Antarctica), *Guaraná Brahma*, *Guaraná Mineiro*, *Guaraná Jesus* (Maranhão regional speciality); these are *carbonated soft drinks* with guaraná-extract flavoring, far lower in caffeine than the traditional preparation and culturally analogous to Coca-Cola but Brazilian.
- *Modern energy drinks:* guaraná is a frequent stimulant component in international energy drinks (Red Bull originally contained guaraná in some formulations; many ongoing brands).
- *Pasta de guaraná as a tonic medicine in caboclo practice:* small daily dose dissolved in water or coffee for stamina; for hunters and rubber-tappers historically the daily morning sustainer.#footnote[Saterê-Mawé canonical preparation: Henman, _Guaraná: A História e o Mito_; Lorenz; the Saterê-Mawé Council's own publications on guaraná production. Commercial Brazilian guaraná soft-drink history: Companhia Antarctica corporate history; Brazilian food-history literature. The pirarucu-tongue grater is documented in ethnographic museum collections and the standard Amazonian ethnographies.]

=== Western / Greco-Roman

No classical tradition. Early-modern naturalists (von Humboldt in his Amazon travels; Martius and Spix in their early-19th-c. Brazilian expeditions; Bates and Wallace in the mid-19th-c. naturalist tradition) recorded the indigenous use, and von Humboldt is sometimes credited with the first European scientific description of guaraná consumption. European pharmacy began to take guaraná seriously in the late 19th c. as a caffeine source (used in _pâte de guarana_ for headache in France). It briefly appeared in European materia medica as an antifatigue / mild stimulant; never a major drug.#footnote[Early-modern European recording: Humboldt and Bonpland's American travels (1799–1804); Martius and Spix's _Reise in Brasilien_ (1823–1831); Henry Walter Bates's _The Naturalist on the River Amazon_ (1863). 19th-c. French pharmacy: the _Codex_ and various 19th-c. _Pharmacopoeia_ entries on _pâte de guarana_ for headache; this is a brief and modest European pharmacy chapter, well documented but minor.]

== Modern Nutrition & Pharmacology

Guaraná seed is a medicinal-stimulant, not a nutrient food.

*Key constituents:*
- *Caffeine* — *2–8% by dry weight* — among the highest natural concentrations known. Tannin-bound (forms complexes with proanthocyanidins).
- *Theobromine* — ~0.02–0.06%.
- *Theophylline* — trace.
- *Tannins* (proanthocyanidins, condensed tannins, catechins, epicatechin) — substantial; modulate caffeine release.
- *Saponins, alkaloids* — minor.
- *Starch* — substantial (the seed is a carbohydrate-rich storage organ).#footnote[Phytochemistry of guaraná: standard pharmacognosy (Evans, _Trease and Evans' Pharmacognosy_); reviews in _Phytotherapy Research_, _Journal of Agricultural and Food Chemistry_, _Brazilian Journal of Pharmacognosy_. The 2–8% caffeine range is well documented and is the source of guaraná's commercial value.]

*Pharmacology of note:*
- *Stimulant action:* the standard CNS-stimulant pharmacology of caffeine — adenosine A1 / A2A receptor antagonism, secondary cAMP and norepinephrine elevations.
- *Prolonged release ("smoother" stimulation):* the tannin-bound caffeine in the traditional preparation absorbs more slowly than coffee's free caffeine; small clinical studies and folk attribution converge on a "longer and smoother" stimulant profile. This is one of guaraná's defensible distinguishing pharmacological features.
- *Cognitive performance:* small clinical studies report modest improvements in attention, alertness, and reaction time; effect sizes modest, consistent with caffeine.
- *Fatigue / mood:* small studies report reductions in subjective fatigue; modest effect.
- *Weight loss:* clinical evidence weak; popular marketing claims overstate.
- *Cardiovascular:* tachycardia, mild blood-pressure elevation at high doses; standard caffeine.
- *Diuretic:* mild, standard caffeine.
- *Antioxidant:* the tannin / catechin fraction is the basis of antioxidant claims; clinical translation preliminary.
- *Hepatotoxicity in energy drinks:* documented in combination-product literature; *not* attributable to guaraná as a single substance at typical doses.
- *Cross-system convergence:* the Saterê-Mawé / Brazilian folk reading of guaraná as an antifatigue stimulant for sustained work is exactly the modern caffeine pharmacology; the prolonged-release feature is a real and documented pharmacological distinction from coffee.#footnote[Clinical evidence: small RCTs and reviews in _Phytotherapy Research_, _Journal of Psychopharmacology_, _Journal of Ethnopharmacology_; verify against current systematic reviews. The "longer and smoother" stimulant profile is supported by modest pharmacokinetic data and consistent folk reports; effect sizes are modest. Combination-product cardiotoxicity: standard cardiology / emergency-medicine literature.]

*Safe daily dosage / upper limit:* The relevant limit is *total caffeine intake*, not raw seed weight.
- *Total caffeine intake — current EFSA / FDA guidance:* up to ~400 mg/day for healthy non-pregnant adults; ≤200 mg/day in pregnancy.
- *Pasta de guaraná (traditional):* ~1–3 g/day of the dried paste yields ~20–240 mg of caffeine depending on caffeine content — well within standard limits, especially in the higher-tannin slow-release form.
- *Standardized guaraná extract:* commonly 75–600 mg/day of seed extract standardized to ~10% caffeine; caffeine load of 7–60 mg per typical capsule.
- *Energy drinks and combination products:* highly variable caffeine load; pay attention to *total* caffeine across the day.
- *Risk groups (avoid or use cautiously):* (a) *pregnancy* — limit; (b) *arrhythmia, hypertension, angina, severe insomnia, panic disorder* — avoid or careful; (c) *peptic ulcer / reflux* — caution; (d) *pediatric* — not appropriate; (e) concurrent stimulant medications (amphetamines, ephedra-class) — avoid combination cardiotoxicity; (f) concurrent MAOI (theoretical); (g) bleeding risk in concert with anticoagulants (mild).
- *Tolerance and dependence:* standard caffeine; tolerance builds over weeks of regular use, dependence and withdrawal headache after cessation are expected.
- *Reference figures, not personalized medical advice.*#footnote[Caffeine guidance: EFSA Scientific Opinion on the safety of caffeine (2015) and FDA / ACOG / NICE pregnancy guidance — verify against current versions, as the regulatory picture is occasionally updated. These are reference thresholds, not personalized medical advice.]

